# Auto-built from Klimek Microanatomy Slide Atlas 2026, pages 5-90 (Labs 1-4, 7)
ATLAS={'lab1': [('atlas/p005_f1', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Parts of the typical student microscope', '', 'Slide Atlas p5 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p006_f1', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Liver, from slide MH 126b Liver on Histology Guide. H, hepatocyte; N, nucleus of hepatocyte; Nu; nuceolus; RBC, red blood cell in a sinusoid (no nucleus); E, nucleus of an endothelial cell in a sinusoid; L, nucleus of a lymphocyte. Note that the lymphocyte has a much darker-staining nucleus than the hepatocyte; this is because more of the chromatin in the lymphocyte is in the form of heterochromatin', '', 'Slide Atlas p6 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p006_f2', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Macrophages in the liver (Kuppfer cells)', '', 'Slide Atlas p6 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p007_f3', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Macrophages in a lymph node (large cells in center of image)', '', 'Slide Atlas p7 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p007_f4', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Pancreas. Ac, acinus; SV, secretory vesicles (zymogen granules); Ap, apical aspect of pancreatic acinar cell (filled with red zymogen granules); Nu, nucleus; Ba, basal part of pancreatic acinar cell –note how basophilic it is due to the RER concentrated here; Eu, euchromatin (dispersed); Hc, heterochromatin. 300X', '', 'Slide Atlas p7 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p008_f5', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Jejunum, showing secretory vesicles in intestinal gland cells. From MH 219 Jejunum', '', 'Slide Atlas p8 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p008_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Fundic glands in the stomach. A, parietal cell with acidophilic cytoplasm due to mitochondria; B, vacuolated area of cytoplasm in a chief cell; C, basophilic area of cytoplasm of a chief cell', '', 'Slide Atlas p8 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p009_f7', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Fundic glands in the stomach, cat. Notice the large round parietal cells with centrally-located nucleus, and eosinophilic cytoplasm, and the smaller, more basophilic chief cells, with eccentrically-located nuclie and a mixture of vacuolated cytoplasm in places, and basophilic cytoplasm in other places', '', 'Slide Atlas p9 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p009_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Tongue, with adipose cells. A, lipid droplet of an adipocyte', '', 'Slide Atlas p9 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p010_f9', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Brown fat (multilocular fat) in the mesentery of the duodenum. This slide demonstrates the appearance of multiple lipid droplets inside each adipocyte. Notice also that the nucleus is not always flattened to the periphery of the cell', '', 'Slide Atlas p10 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p010_f10', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Epidermis from thick skin', '', 'Slide Atlas p10 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p011_f11', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Melanin in basal layer of epidermis of thick skin. The brown material in the cells of the more basal layers is melanin', '', 'Slide Atlas p11 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p011_f12', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Mitotic figures in an ovarian follicle. A, anaphase; B, prophase', '', 'Slide Atlas p11 · Figure 12', 0), ('atlas/p012_f13', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Prophase. 600X', '', 'Slide Atlas p12 · Figure 13', 0), ('atlas/p012_f14', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Metaphase. 600X', '', 'Slide Atlas p12 · Figure 14', 0), ('atlas/p013_f15', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Metaphase in a cell in an intestinal gland. From MHS 219 Jejunum, the Histology Guide', '', 'Slide Atlas p13 · Figure 15', 0), ('atlas/p013_f16', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Anaphase in an intestinal epithelial cell', '', 'Slide Atlas p13 · Figure 16', 0), ('atlas/p014_f17', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Telophase. 600X', '', 'Slide Atlas p14 · Figure 17', 0), ('atlas/p014_f18', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Example an epithelium consisting of uniform, columnar cells, and of a curved tubular structure with a (mostly) uniform diameter, both sectioned in various ways. The outline of the structure on the slide and the appearance of the epithelium look different depending on the orientation of the knife cut relative to the epithelium (plane of section). From . and', '', 'Slide Atlas p14 · Figure 18', 0), ('atlas/p015_f19', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Two different areas of the same colon slide. The glands are straight and tubular. On the left they are cut in a transverse plane of section. On the right they are cut in a longitudinal plane of section', '', 'Slide Atlas p15 · Figure 19', 0)], 'lab2': [('atlas/p016_f1', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Fetal pig head frontal section to demonstrate mesenchyme. Mesenchyme (M) fills areas between developing bone and other tissues', '', 'Slide Atlas p16 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p016_f2', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Area of mesenchyme between developing bone spicules', '', 'Slide Atlas p16 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p017_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Mesenchyme with detail of mesenchymal cells. M, nucleus of mesenchymal cell; C, cytoplasmic process of mesenchymal cell; E, extracellular matrix', '', 'Slide Atlas p17 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p017_f4', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Cells and fibers in loose irregular (areolar) connective tissue of the mesentery. Fibroblasts have elongated nuclei. Macrophages have large, oval, pale nuclei. Other cell nuclei are difficult to distinguish. Elastic fibers are darkly stained and thread-like. Collagen fibers are larger and stain pale pink', '', 'Slide Atlas p17 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p018_f5', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Lamina propria of the colon. LP, lamina propria (loose connective tissue filled with defensive cells)', '', 'Slide Atlas p18 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p018_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Various cells of connective tissue in a salivary gland. A, macrophage nucleus; B, plasma cell; C, collagen fiber; D, fibroblast nucleus; E, eosinophil', '', 'Slide Atlas p18 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p019_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Connective tissue of the skin of the axilla. P, plasma cells; L, lymphocyte (only see nucleus); F, fibroblast; M, mast cell; BV, blood vessel; E, endothelial cell', '', 'Slide Atlas p19 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p019_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Lamina propria of the esophagus. P, typical plasma cells; M, Mott cell. This slide is described as showing inflammation', '', 'Slide Atlas p19 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p020_f9', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Mott cell (M) in the dermis', '', 'Slide Atlas p20 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p020_f10', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Tonsil, showing lymphocytes deep to and in the epithelium. BM, basement membrane (location); EP, epithelium of tonsil; P, plasma cell; L, lymphocyte (the vast majority of the cells in the connective tissue are lymphocytes); M, macrophage; P, plasma cell', '', 'Slide Atlas p20 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p021_f11', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'High magnification of lamina propria between glands of the colon. M, nuclei of macrophages; L, lymphocyte nucleus; P, plasma cell; E, eosinophil', '', 'Slide Atlas p21 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p021_f12', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Neutrophils in connective tissue, MH 130a Gallbladder, Histology Guide. Neutrophils (arrows) are seen in loose connective tissue', '', 'Slide Atlas p21 · Figure 12', 0), ('atlas/p022_f13', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Reticular connective tissue, lymph node, silver stain. The dark fibers are ‘agyrophilic’ (silver loving) reticular fibers. 150X', '', 'Slide Atlas p22 · Figure 13', 0), ('atlas/p022_f14', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Layers of the back of the eye. BV, blood vessel; P, melanin pigment in the connective tissue of the choroid; PE, pigmented epithelium of the retina. 150X', '', 'Slide Atlas p22 · Figure 14', 0), ('atlas/p022_f15', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Loose connective tissue with adipocytes. A, lipid droplet filling adipocyte; N, nucleus of adipocyte; C, collagen fibers', '', 'Slide Atlas p22 · Figure 15', 0), ('atlas/p023_f16', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Dense irregular connective tissue of the tunica albuginea, penis', '', 'Slide Atlas p23 · Figure 16', 0), ('atlas/p023_f17', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Layers of the skin of the palm. E, epidermis; D, dermis; H, hypodermis (extends beyond field of view). The dermis is dense irregular connective tissue; the hypodermis is largely adipose tissue', '', 'Slide Atlas p23 · Figure 17', 0), ('atlas/p024_f18', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Tendon and adjacent skeletal muscle cut in longitudinal section. T, tendon; F, fibrocyte nucleus; SM, skeletal muscle. Note the parallel bundles of collagen', '', 'Slide Atlas p24 · Figure 18', 0), ('atlas/p024_f19', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Tendon in oblique section (left) and longitudinal section (right). F, fibrocyte nucleus', '', 'Slide Atlas p24 · Figure 19', 0), ('atlas/p025_f20', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Dense regular connective tissue in a developing tendon. C, collagen fibers; F, fibroblast nuclei; T, tendon', '', 'Slide Atlas p25 · Figure 20', 0), ('atlas/p025_f21', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Nuchal ligament, trichrome stain. E, elastic fibers (brown); C, collagen fibers (blue)', '', 'Slide Atlas p25 · Figure 21', 0), ('atlas/p026_f22', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Wall of aorta, an elastic artery, stained with H&E', '', 'Slide Atlas p26 · Figure 22', 0), ('atlas/p026_f23', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Wall of the aorta, stained with Aldehyde Fuschin. Elastid layers are dark', '', 'Slide Atlas p26 · Figure 23', 0), ('atlas/p053_f1', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Surface view of mesothelium. Note ‘cobblestone’ appearance. 100X', '', 'Slide Atlas p53 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p053_f2', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Transverse section of mesothelium on the uterine tube. M, mesothelium (simple squamous epithelium); L, loose connective tissue; SM, smooth muscle', '', 'Slide Atlas p53 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p053_f3', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Pair of capillaries, showing nuclei of endothelial cells', '', 'Slide Atlas p53 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p054_f4', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Thyroid follicle lined by cuboidal epithelium', '', 'Slide Atlas p54 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p054_f5', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Stratified cuboidal epithelium. SC, stratified cuboidal epithelium in the duct of a gland in the esophagus; SS, simple squamous epithelium in a vein', '', 'Slide Atlas p54 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p055_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Epithelial lining between adjacent villi, small intestine. Simple columnar epithelium with a brush border and goblet cells. GC, goblet cell cytoplasm; GCN, goblet cell nucleus; BB, brush border; ECN, epithelial cell nucleus', '', 'Slide Atlas p55 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p055_f7', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Ciliated and non-ciliated cells in a simple columnar epithelium cells', '', 'Slide Atlas p55 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p056_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Electron microscopic comparison of cilia and microvilli. C, cilia; M, microvilli', '', 'Slide Atlas p56 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p056_f9', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Respiratory epithelium in the trachea (pseudostratified, columnar, ciliated with goblet cells)', '', 'Slide Atlas p56 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p057_f10', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Respiratory epithelium in the trachea. The trachea is lined by pseudostratified columnar epithelium. Note that all the surface cells have cilia at their free (apical) surface. Mucus-secreting unicellular gland cells ("goblet cells") are interspersed among the ciliated cells', '', 'Slide Atlas p57 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p057_f11', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Stratified squamous non-keratinized epithelium of the esophagus. Nuclei are retained to the most superficial layer', '', 'Slide Atlas p57 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p058_f12', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Stratified squamous epithelium, keratinized. With keratinized stratified squamous epithelia, you can see all the following layers: B, stratum basale (germinal cells); S, stratum spinosum; G, stratum granulosum; L, stratum lucidum (only seen in some regions); C, stratum corneum (flat, anucleate, dead cells packed with keratin). The stratum corneum usually appears more eosinophilic (red) than it does here. 300X', '', 'Slide Atlas p58 · Figure 12', 0), ('atlas/p058_f13', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Transitional epithelium. S, surface cell (note how it bulges into the lumen and the nucleus is not flat as it would be with stratified squamous epithelium). 600x', '', 'Slide Atlas p58 · Figure 13', 0), ('atlas/p059_f14', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Transitional epithelium of the ureter, human', '', 'Slide Atlas p59 · Figure 14', 0), ('atlas/p060_f1', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Glands and ducts in three dimensions and in cross-section. A. striated duct B. intercalated duct C. mucous acinus D. serous acinus E. serous demilune F. myoepithelial cells G. basal lamina H. terminal bars (Redrawn and modified from Junqueira, Carneiro and Kelley: Basic Histology, 7th Edition, Appleton & Lange, Norwalk, 1992)', '', 'Slide Atlas p60 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p060_f2', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Small intestine, luminal space to the right. C, crypt (of Lieberkuhn) or intestinal gland, a simple tubular gland opening to the surface between the finger-like projections called villi (V). C’, crypt cut in cross section (often the case because you seldom get a perfect cut so the simple tubular gland is shown along its entire length). 38X', '', 'Slide Atlas p60 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p061_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Sweat glands. A, adipose cell; C, collagen; S, merocrine sweat gland. 38X', '', 'Slide Atlas p61 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p061_f4', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Human scalp. D, dermis; Du, duct of sweat gland; Ha, hair follicle; E, eccrine (merocrine) sweat gland; Hy, hypodermis; S, sebaceous gland. Taken from MH 086b Scalp Histology Guide', '', 'Slide Atlas p61 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p062_f5', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Human scalp, showing a sebaceous gland, a hair follicle, and sweat gland ducts', '', 'Slide Atlas p62 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p062_f6', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Uterus, with long straight glands that become more dilated and coiled throughout the cycle', '', 'Slide Atlas p62 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p063_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Submucosal glands of the trachea. S, serous cells; D, duct; M, mucous cells', '', 'Slide Atlas p63 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p063_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Mixed seromucous salivary gland. A portion of one lobule is shown. Ducts within lobules are intralobular (a positional description only). D, striated duct; SD, serous demilune; S, serous acinus; M, mucous acinus', '', 'Slide Atlas p63 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p064_f9', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Striated intralobular duct, high magnification, in salivary gland. 600X', '', 'Slide Atlas p64 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p064_f10', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Pancreas. P, Pacinian corpuscle; A, acinus (serous). 38X', '', 'Slide Atlas p64 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p065_f11', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Pancreas. Taken from DMS142 Pancreas, Human on the LUMEN site', '', 'Slide Atlas p65 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p065_f12', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Mammary gland, active. A, alveolus; D, duct (note connective tissue beside it). 150X', '', 'Slide Atlas p65 · Figure 12', 0), ('atlas/p066_f13', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Active mammary gland, human', '', 'Slide Atlas p66 · Figure 13', 0), ('atlas/p067_f1', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Thick skin. A, epidermis; B, stratum granulosum; C, stratum spinosum; D, stratum basale; E, dermis (approx. ); F, hypodermis (approx. ); G, artery; H, eccrine sweat gland; I, adipose tissue', '', 'Slide Atlas p67 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p068_f2', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Skin layers. From MH 091ahr Thick Skin in Histology Guide. Arrows in right image demonstrate gaps between cells of the stratum spinosum', '', 'Slide Atlas p68 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p068_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Structures of the dermis and hypodermis. From MH 091 Thick Skin in Histology Guide. P, Pacinian corpuscle; N, nerve; BV, blood vessels', '', 'Slide Atlas p68 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p069_f4', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Thin skin, scalp. A, hair follicles; B, piloerector muscles; C, dermis; D, hypodermis; E, blood vessel; F, eccrine sweat gland (in domestic animals these are located on non-hairy skin); G, sebaceous gland. Notice that blood vessels can look like sweat glands at low magnification', '', 'Slide Atlas p69 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p069_f5', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', "External ear skin, Verhoeff's elastic stain. A hair follicle with an associated sebaceous gland and apocrine sweat gland is shown. Right image shows detail of the apocrine sweat gland; notice the evidence of apocrine secretion", '', 'Slide Atlas p69 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p070_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Hair follicle, longitudinal section. A, hair; B, inner root sheath; C, outer root sheath; D, connective tissue sheath; E, position of cuticle (not visible here)', '', 'Slide Atlas p70 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p070_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Hair follicle, longitudinal section. P, papilla; C, connective tissue sheath; O, outer root sheath; I, inner root sheath; H, hair', '', 'Slide Atlas p70 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p071_f8', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Image of a monkey lip, and a tactile hair', '', 'Slide Atlas p71 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p071_f9', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Comparison of histological and gross anatomy sections of tactile hairs', '', 'Slide Atlas p71 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p072_f10', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Tactile hair. O, outer connective tissue sheath; V, venous sinus; I, inner connective tissue sheath; G, glassy membrane; E, external root sheath; S, sebaceous gland. 150X', '', 'Slide Atlas p72 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p073_f11', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Equine hoof. Bottom image is an enlargement of area in box in top image. A, hoof tubule; B, intertubular horn; C, primary dermal lamina; D, primary epidermal lamina; E, secondary epidermal lamina; F, secondary dermal lamina. From . (VIBS Histology, Texas A&M)', '', 'Slide Atlas p73 · Figure 11', 0)], 'lab3': [('atlas/p027_f1', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Fetal manus, developing hyaline cartilage of the proximal phalanx. A, mesenchyme around the developing digit; B, center of condrification for the cartilage model for the second phalanx; C, the cartilage model of the first phalanx; D, a mitotic figure in the cartilage model of the first phalanx', '', 'Slide Atlas p27 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p027_f2', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Trachea, human. TM, tracheal mucosa; SM, smooth muscle (trachealis muscle); G, glands; PC, perichondrium; HCR, hyaline cartilage ring; connective tissue, connective tissue', '', 'Slide Atlas p27 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p028_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Cartilage, trachea, human. PC, perichondrium; F, fibrous layer; CL, chondrogenic layer; CB, chondroblasts; C, chondrocyte in lacuna; EDM, extracellular matrix; IG, isogenous groups', '', 'Slide Atlas p28 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p028_f4', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Developing endochondral bone. A, joint space (articular cartilage on either side); B, epiphysis, entirely cartilaginous still; C, physis (growth plate); D, marrow cavity with hemopoietic tissue; E, periosteum; F, bone collar', '', 'Slide Atlas p28 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p029_f5', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Joint in human fetal finger. A, B, epiphyses of two bones; C, synovial membrane (found inside the fibrous joint capsule but not covering the articular surface); D, articular surface of the cartilage; E, physis', '', 'Slide Atlas p29 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p029_f6', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Articular cartilage (hyaline cartilage without a perichondrium). Note the slightly flattened chondrocytes of the superficial layer and the cells arranged in columns perpendicular to the joint surface', '', 'Slide Atlas p29 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p030_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', "External ear, human, Iodinated iron-hematoxylin stain (Verhoeff's elastic stain). P, perichondrium; EF, elastic fibers; C, chondrocytes", '', 'Slide Atlas p30 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p030_f8', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Elastic cartilage stained with H&E. Elastic fibers stain pink and are visible against the blue-stained ground substance', '', 'Slide Atlas p30 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p031_f9', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Fibrocartilage', '', 'Slide Atlas p31 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p031_f10', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Symphysis pubis. FC, fibrocartilage; FCT, fibrous connective tissue; HC, hyaline cartilage; B, bone', '', 'Slide Atlas p31 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p032_f1', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Scanning electron microscopy image of an osteon (purple) surrounded by portions of other osteons (blue). Slide EM 217 Osteon on the Histology Guide', '', 'Slide Atlas p32 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p032_f2', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Cross section of the distal portion of an equine radius, showing both compact and spongy bone', '', 'Slide Atlas p32 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p033_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Partial cross section of end of a long bone, from MH 043 Cancellous and Compact Bone on the Histology Guide. 1, periosteum; 2, compact bone; 3, spongy bone containing trabeculae', '', 'Slide Atlas p33 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p033_f4', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Lamellar bone, from MH 044 Ground Bone on The Histology Guide. 1, outer circumferential lamellae; 2, compact bone containing osteons; 3, inner circumferential lamellae', '', 'Slide Atlas p33 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p034_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', "Lamellar bone, Schmorl's stain, from MHS 202 Ground Bone on The Histology Guide. 1, central canal; 2, cement (reversal) line; 3, perforating (Volkman’s) canals; 4, lamellae of the osteon; 5, interstitial lamellae; 6, lamellae from either inner or outer circumferential lamellae", '', 'Slide Atlas p34 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p035_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', "Lamellar bone, Schmorl's stain, from MHS 202 Ground Bone on The Histology Guide. La, lacuna (containing an osteocyte cell body in live bone); C, canaliculi (containing cytoplasmic processes of osteocytes – note how they are communicating here); CC, Central (Haversian) canal (containing blood vessels, nerves, connective tissue); E, endosteum; L, lamella", '', 'Slide Atlas p35 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p035_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Osteocyte in a lacuna as seen with scanning electron microscopy, from EM 218 Osteocyte of the Histology Guide. 1, canaliculus; 2, osteocyte process; 3, mineralized bone tissue; 4, unmineralized bone tissue (osteoid); 5, osteocyte; 6, lacuna', '', 'Slide Atlas p35 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p036_f9', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Woven (immature or primary) bone formed by intramembranous ossification in mesenchymal tissue. From slide MHS 242 in the Histology Guide', '', 'Slide Atlas p36 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p036_f10', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Bone cells. OC, osteocyte; O, osteoclast; H, Howship’s lacuna. 600X', '', 'Slide Atlas p36 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p037_f11', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Developing bone. and . C, compact bone (from remodeling of bone collar); P, periosteum; BC, bone collar; T, tendons; S, spicules; EP, epiphyseal plate (physis); E, epiphysis', '', 'Slide Atlas p37 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p038_f12', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Developing endochondral bone. A, joint space (articular cartilage on either side); B, epiphysis, entirely cartilaginous still; C, physis (growth plate); D, marrow cavity with hemopoietic tissue; E, periosteum; F, bone collar', '', 'Slide Atlas p38 · Figure 12', 0), ('atlas/p038_f13', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Developing Endochondral bone, physis. A, calcified cartilage ECM; B, lacuna with dead chondrocyte; C, osteoid (beginning to organize into the spicules); D, osteoblast; E, osteocyte in a lacuna; F, lumen of invading capillary; G, osteoprogenitor and other cells arriving with capillary. Growth direction is towards upper left', '', 'Slide Atlas p38 · Figure 13', 0), ('atlas/p039_f14', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Advancing ossification in growth plate. C, chondrocytes in lacunae; OC, osteoclasts; OL, osteocyte in a lacuna; O, osteoid', '', 'Slide Atlas p39 · Figure 14', 0), ('atlas/p040_f15', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Developing tibia in cross section. A, enlarging bone collar forming from periosteum; B, spicules in developing marrow cavity; dotted line, approximate location of first layer of bone collar; C, developing periosteum; D, fibrous layer of periosteum; E, cellular layer of periosteum; F, osteoblasts in endosteum; G, osteocytes in lacunae; H, hyaline cartilage extracellular matrix. From . 048_HISTO_40X.htm', '', 'Slide Atlas p40 · Figure 15', 0), ('atlas/p041_f1', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Spinal cord and meninges. DF, LF, VF, dorsal, lateral and ventral funiculi; DM, dura mater; A, arachnoid; DR, VR, dorsal and ventral roots; DRG, dorsal root ganglion; DH, VH, dorsal and ventral horns; C, central canal; LMN, lower motor neurons; VMF, ventral median fissure', '', 'Slide Atlas p41 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p041_f2', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Alpha motor neurons, ventral horn, gray matter, spinal cord. N, Nissl substance (RER); H, axon hillock (note lack of nissl substance); NP, neuropil; Nu, nucleolus; O, nuclei of oligodendrocytes', '', 'Slide Atlas p41 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p042_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Gray matter, ventral horn, spinal cord, glial cells. A, astrocyte; O, oligodendrocyte; N, nucleolus of neuron; M, microglial cell', '', 'Slide Atlas p42 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p042_f4', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Dorsal root ganglion. A, axon hillock of a pseudounipolar sensory neuron; S, satellite cell nuclei; N, nucleus of a sensory neuron', '', 'Slide Atlas p42 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p043_f5', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'A section of a dorsal root ganglion demonstrating myelinated axons (center bottom of image) passing through the ganglion adjacent to the large sensory neuron', '', 'Slide Atlas p43 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p043_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Peripheral nerve, cross section, Masson stain. E, epineurium; P, perineurium', '', 'Slide Atlas p43 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p044_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Peripheral nerve, cross section, Masson stain. Ep, epineurium; P, perineurium; En, endoneurium; A, axon', '', 'Slide Atlas p44 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p044_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Myelinated nerve. N, node of Ranvier; A, axons; MS, myelin space', '', 'Slide Atlas p44 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p045_f9', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Myelinated axon, special stain. N, node of Ranvier; I, internode. 600X', '', 'Slide Atlas p45 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p046_f11', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Peripheral nerves. Typical appearances of a peripheral nerve on an H&E stained slide. Many of the nuclei within the nerve belong to Schwann cells; others are fibroblasts of the endoneurium', '', 'Slide Atlas p46 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p046_f12', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Parasympathetic ganglion, salivary gland. The parasympathetic ganglion looks very similar to a sympathetic ganglion. A, neuron cell body surrounded by satellite cells; B, neuron nucleus', '', 'Slide Atlas p46 · Figure 12', 0)], 'lab4': [('atlas/p047_f1', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Skeletal muscle. Striations and peripheral nuclei are clearly visible', '', 'Slide Atlas p47 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p047_f2', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Skeletal muscle. P, perimysium; M, myofiber in cross section. Note granular appearance and peripheral nuclei. Each cluster of myofibers is a ‘fasciculus.’ 150X', '', 'Slide Atlas p47 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p048_f3', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Muscle spindle in perimysium, surrounded by skeletal muscle fibers. From slide MHS 262 on the Histology Guide', '', 'Slide Atlas p48 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p048_f4', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Muscle spindle in longitunidal/oblique section of muscle, surrounded by skeletal muscle fibers. From slide MHS 030 on the Histology Guide', '', 'Slide Atlas p48 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p049_f5', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Myoneural junction. M, motor endplate. The whole figure represents a ‘motor unit,’ which is one alpha motor neuron, its axon and all the myofibers it supplies. 600X', '', 'Slide Atlas p49 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p049_f6', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Smooth muscle, monkey jejunum. This slide shows smooth muscle cut in both longitudinal and transverse section', '', 'Slide Atlas p49 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p050_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Wall of ileum. MP, Myenteric plexus; C, circular layer of muscle (cut in cross section); L, longitudinal layer of muscle (cut in longitudinal section); Se, serosa; Su, submucosa', '', 'Slide Atlas p50 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p050_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Smooth muscle in the small intestine. From MH 116 of the Histology Guide. 1, smooth muscle in the lamina muscularis mucosa; 2, smooth muscle in an arteriole, demonstrating more obvious outlines of individual cells', '', 'Slide Atlas p50 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p051_f9', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Cardiac muscle. Fascicles on the right are in longitudinal section, those on the left are in cross section. N, nuclei; I, intercalated disc', '', 'Slide Atlas p51 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p051_f10', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Purkinje fibers near the inner heart surface. Purkinje fibers (left) and cardiac myocytes (right). From slide MHS 245 Heart on the Histology Guide', '', 'Slide Atlas p51 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p052_f11', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Section of heart. E, endocardium; P, Purkinje fibers; M, cardiac muscle cut in cross section', '', 'Slide Atlas p52 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p074_f1', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Cardiac muscle in longitudinal section, demonstrating brancing and intercalated disks. A, intercalated disk; B, branching of myocytes; C, centrally-located nuclei; D, connective tissue nuclei', '', 'Slide Atlas p74 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p074_f2', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Cardiac muscle, with Purkinje fibers near endocardium. E, endocardium (no fat, in contrast to epicardium); P, layer of Purkinje fibers; M, myocardium. Compared to regular cardiac myocytes, Purkinje fibers are more “swollen”, so they appear larger and less dense. Intercalated disks and centrally-located nuclei are seen in Purkinje fibers', '', 'Slide Atlas p74 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p075_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Purkinje fibers in the myocardium (close to endocardium). P, Purkinje fibers. 150X', '', 'Slide Atlas p75 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p075_f4', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Cardiac muscle in longitudinal (top) and cross (bottom) section. Notice the relatively small, branching cells, with centrally located nuclei. In cross section cardiac muscle appears granular due to the myofibrils. Flattened nuclei between cells are mainly either those of fibroblasts or endothelial cells. Loose connective tissue surrounds the cells', '', 'Slide Atlas p75 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p076_f5', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Aortic wall. E, endothelium; M, tunica media; L, elastic lamina; Ex, external elastic lamina, A, adventitia. Note how difficult it is to visualize the internal elastic lamina (although in this section it is easier to see than usual) – it blends in with all the other elastic fibers', '', 'Slide Atlas p76 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p076_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Layers of the wall of a the aorta. L, lumen; M, tunica media; A, adventitia; a, arteriole of vasa vasorum; v, venule of vasa vasorum', '', 'Slide Atlas p76 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p077_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Layers of a muscular artery. E, endothelial cell nucleus; I, tunica intima; M, tunica media; A, tunica adventitia', '', 'Slide Atlas p77 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p077_f8', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Tunica intima of a muscular artery. E, endothelial cell nuclei; SE, sub-endothelial connective tissue; IEL, internal elastic lamina; SM, smooth muscle of tunica media', '', 'Slide Atlas p77 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p078_f9', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Muscular artery (special stain to demonstrate elastin). IEL, internal elastic lamina; TM, tunica media; EEL, external elastic lamina; TA, tunica adventitia. The tunica adventitia has a mixture of collagen (orange) and elastin (black) fibers. Note that even in the muscular artery there is some elastin in the tunica media', '', 'Slide Atlas p78 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p078_f10', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Vein (left) adjacent to a muscular artery (right). The tunica media is much thinner in the vein and the tunica adventitia is the thickest part of the wall', '', 'Slide Atlas p78 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p079_f11', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Layers of an arteriole. IEL, internal elastic lamina; SM, nucleus of a smooth muscle cell; E, nucleus of an endothelial cell; TA, tunica adventitia', '', 'Slide Atlas p79 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p079_f12', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Capillaries in loose connective tissue. C, cross section; L, longitudinal section; S, smooth muscle cell nucleus. 300X', '', 'Slide Atlas p79 · Figure 12', 0), ('atlas/p080_f13', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Capillaries containing red blood cells. Several capillaries containing RBC’s are seen in various planes of section', '', 'Slide Atlas p80 · Figure 13', 0), ('atlas/p080_f14', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Small vessels in loose connective tissue. LC, loose connective tissue; V, venule (note the irregular outline and the scattered muscle cells); L, lymph vessel (note the lack of structure to the wall except for the endothelial cells; C, capillary; A, arteriole in oblique section', '', 'Slide Atlas p80 · Figure 14', 0), ('atlas/p081_f15', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Small vessels in the medulla of a lymph node. V, venule; A, arteriole; L, lymph vessel. Note the RBC’s in the arteriole and venule, and the absence of RBC’s in the lymph vessel', '', 'Slide Atlas p81 · Figure 15', 0), ('atlas/p081_f16', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Lymph vessel with valve, consisting only of a double layer of endothelium', '', 'Slide Atlas p81 · Figure 16', 0)], 'lab7': [('atlas/p082_f1', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Human appendix with lymph nodules and diffuse lymphatic tissue (far right side). H&E stain. N, lymph nodule; GC, germinal center; SM, tunica submucosa; TM, tunica mucosa; TS, tunica serosa', '', 'Slide Atlas p82 · Figure 1', 0), ('atlas/p083_f2', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Peyer’s patch (group of lymph nodules) in the small intestine. From', '', 'Slide Atlas p83 · Figure 2', 0), ('atlas/p083_f3', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Palatine tonsil, human, low magnification, showing epithelium, diffuse lymphatic tissue, and nodules. E, epithelium; N, lymph nodules; L, lymphocytes invading epithelium (hallmark of a tonsil)', '', 'Slide Atlas p83 · Figure 3', 0), ('atlas/p084_f4', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Palatine tonsil, human, showing lymphocytes in the epithelium. SSE, stratified squamous epithelium; BM, approximate position of basement membrane; L, lymphocytes invading epithelium (hallmark of a tonsil)', '', 'Slide Atlas p84 · Figure 4', 0), ('atlas/p084_f5', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Monkey lymph node demonstrating capsule, cortex and medulla, and placement of nodules. C, capsule; S, subcapsular sinus; N, nodules in the cortex; M, medulla (containing cords and sinuses); PC, paracortex (deep cortex)', '', 'Slide Atlas p84 · Figure 5', 0), ('atlas/p085_f6', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Monkey lymph node, section from medulla near hilus. N, nodule at edge of cortex; C, medullary cord; S, medullary sinus; EL, efferent lymphatic vessels, some with valves. Note the communication of the sinuses with the efferent vessels at the asterisk', '', 'Slide Atlas p85 · Figure 6', 0), ('atlas/p085_f7', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Lymph node, pup. C, capsule; T, trabecula; T’, trabecular sinus; S, subcapsular sinus; N, secondary nodule in cortex (note also diffuse lymphatic tissue). The dotted yellow line represents the paracortex (thymus dependent zone); note the medullary cords of lymphatic tissue extending down into the medulla. This is an underdeveloped lymph node from a 3 week old pup. Note that the cortex and medulla are poorly populated with lymphocytes. 38X', '', 'Slide Atlas p85 · Figure 7', 0), ('atlas/p086_f8', 'What is shown here? Name the tissue, cell, or structure.', 'Pig lymph node, schematic. The key thing to look for is the presence of nodules in the medulla (there should be none in the typical lymph node)', '', 'Slide Atlas p86 · Figure 8', 0), ('atlas/p086_f9', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Hemal node. T, trabecula. The hemal node is much smaller than a regular lymph node. 38X', '', 'Slide Atlas p86 · Figure 9', 0), ('atlas/p087_f10', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Spleen. A, splenic sinus in red pulp; B, trabecula with blood vessel; C, nodule, D, central arteriole surrounded by periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS)', '', 'Slide Atlas p87 · Figure 10', 0), ('atlas/p087_f11', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Spleen, demonstrating regions of splenic tissue. RP, red pulp (splenic cords and sinuses); N, nodule (peripheral white pulp); A, arteriole; periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS) , periarteriolar lymphoid sheath surrounding arteriole; V, vein', '', 'Slide Atlas p87 · Figure 11', 0), ('atlas/p088_f12', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'A splenic nodule adjacent to a central artery, surrounded by red pulp. RP, red pulp; M, marginal zone; CA, central artery; GC, germinal center', '', 'Slide Atlas p88 · Figure 12', 0), ('atlas/p088_f13', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Spleen red and white pulp. 1, sinuses of the red pulp; 2, periarteriolar lymphoid sheath (PALS) around central arteriole; 3, nodule with germinal center; 4, splenic cords', '', 'Slide Atlas p88 · Figure 13', 0), ('atlas/p089_f14', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Spleen, demonstrating relatively empty splenic sinuses. S, sinuses (some with blood; some cleared); C, cords; B, blood in a sinus; N, neutrophils in circulation and in a cord; M, nucleus of a macrophage', '', 'Slide Atlas p89 · Figure 14', 0), ('atlas/p089_f15', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Lobule of the thymus, demonstrating cortex and medulla. Taken from MH 079 Thymus of the Histology Guide. 1, septum; 2, thymic corpuscle; 3, medulla; 4, cortex', '', 'Slide Atlas p89 · Figure 15', 0), ('atlas/p090_f16', 'Name each labeled structure (letters/numbers), then name the tissue or organ.', 'Medulla of the thymus, with prominent corpuscles. M, medulla; C, cortex; E, nuclei of epithelial cells (form the stroma); TC, Thymic corpuscle', '', 'Slide Atlas p90 · Figure 16', 0)]}
